<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<kml v="1" site="game-lab.custom" epoch="1">
  <head>
    <title>Game lab</title>
    <meta name="description" content="A playable WebAssembly game running inside the Kickl sandbox."/>
    <meta name="headline" content="A real game, running in a sandbox with no network, no clock and no files."/>
    <meta name="tags" content="games, wasm, sandbox, demo"/>
  </head>
  <body>
    <page>
      <h1>Game lab</h1>
      <badge>webassembly · no imports · fuel-metered</badge>

      <p>The game below is a compiled WebAssembly module sealed into this page like any other blob.
      It does not start until you press play. It cannot reach the network, the disk, the clipboard
      or a clock: the browser hands it a seed, your keys and a 240 by 160 picture to draw on, and
      nothing else.</p>

      <game id="catcher" label="Catcher"/>

      <h2>How it is kept safe</h2>
      <p>Every frame runs on a fixed instruction budget, so an endless loop simply stops the game.
      Its memory is capped. It must declare zero imports or the browser refuses it. When it stops, you
      see why. The best score it keeps is stored encrypted in your own profile, never on the page.</p>

      <quote>A game is arithmetic on its own memory. Anything else is a request the host never answers.</quote>
    </page>
  </body>
</kml>
